[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A view is a tree of narratives: rows in a statement, Cites for what it presents, Contains for its parts

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-NARRATIVE-IS-THE-VIEW-NODE
TITLE: A view is a tree of narratives: rows in a statement, Cites for what it presents, Contains for its parts
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A view needs a unit smaller than a narrative that must be addressable by UID, and promoting the row to its own narrative proves too costly in practice.
STATEMENT: >>>
A rendered view is a tree of NARRATIVE nodes and nothing else.

THE ROOT is a narrative nothing Contains. Its children, in the order its
RELATIONS block lists them, are the view's tabs; a tab's children are its
sections; a section's children are cards. Contains is a Child relation
written on the container, so the parts stay untouched, order lives in one
place, and a narrative is never a dependency of anything.

A LINE of the view is a ROW inside a narrative whose WIDGET is rows: a "- "
item in the STATEMENT, optionally ending in a bracket "[WORD]" or
"[WORD: by]" whose word comes from the root's legend. A row is not a node.
It is promoted to a narrative of its own by hand when it needs an identity
-- to be cited, fingerprinted, or contained elsewhere -- and not before.

WHAT A NARRATIVE PRESENTS it Cites: a Parent relation, written on the
narrative, to each node whose wording it depends on, with a PARENT_FP entry
per citation so the edge goes dirty when the cited node changes. A narrative
that is rewritten to present different nodes changes its own edges; the
presented nodes never change. Links inside prose are strictdoc's own
inline link token (LINK, in square brackets, then the UID), in a STATEMENT and never in a TITLE, so a dangling one
refuses the export.

WHERE A NARRATIVE LIVES is where what it cites lives: Cites is a Parent
relation, so INV-NO-EXTERNAL-PLAN-REFS forbids one into another plan. A view
over a plan sits in that plan's directory and is collected with it; a view
over the spec is durable; a view that must mention another plan's nodes does
so with inline links only.

A narrative carries DEPTH and PARENT_FP like the claim types, so readiness,
signing and the fingerprint check need no special case; DEPTH on a narrative
reads sketch for a draft and interface-settled once the operator has read it
and let it stand.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Three designs were judged against the operator's priorities and a skeptic's
attack on 2026-08-30. Lines as nodes (one file per line) was rejected as the
five-times-effort design; lines as rows carries the existing whiteboard
verbatim and keeps the file count at one per screen. Child on the container
was chosen over Parent on each part because the export preserves RELATIONS
order, so no ORDER counter exists anywhere, and because nothing then depends
on a narrative. Parent for citation was chosen because a fingerprint must be
backed by a Parent relation on the node that carries it.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled 2026-08-30 as a first-pass decision under the operator's delegation of that day ("I'll trust you to take first pass at making decisions"); overrule by superseding. AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has no flag for the human rungs. The strictdoc measurements this rests on: RELATIONS order is preserved in the JSON export; a Child written on A registers a derived parent link on the target in strictdoc's own index (harmless within one plan); an inline link inside a multiline field is resolved and refused if dangling, while one inside TITLE is inert text.
<<<
